WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our first guest tonight
is a very talented actress

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.436
and director.
She directs and stars in

00:00:04.436 --> 00:00:05.805
"What Happens Later,"

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:07.373
which is in theaters
November 3rd.

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:08.508
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:11.343
-Well, I always wondered
why you left.

00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:12.912
-No, you didn't.
-Yeah.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.914
-We talked about it all
before I left Madison.

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:16.482
We sat in the back booth
at Morty's

00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:17.716
over our fries and beers,

00:00:17.716 --> 00:00:20.653
and we very mature,
very civilized about it.

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.555
Did a whole postmortem.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.257
Come on.
You know that's true.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:27.927
-No.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.128
When people break up,
there's the thing

00:00:29.128 --> 00:00:30.229
that they tell each other,

00:00:30.229 --> 00:00:31.598
there's the thing
they tell their friends

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:34.200
and then --
then there's the truth.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.936
-Please welcome to the show.
Meg Ryan, everybody.

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:39.305
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:54.487 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:57.357
I am so happy
to have you here.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:00.093
-Hi, Seth.
-This is a thrill for me.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:01.327
This is also very exciting.

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:02.728
You don't just star
in this film.

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:03.930
You wrote
and directed this film.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.365
-Yeah.
-How did this story

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:06.800
first come to you?

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.902
-Well, it was a play that the
playwright did a first draft of,

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:14.173
and I -- it was during Covid,
and I had a lot of time,

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:16.308
and I just started writing,
and I kept writing

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.110
and I kept writing.
And at like 5:00 in the morning

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.446
I'd get more ideas
and more ideas.

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:23.482
David Duchovny is in the movie
with me, too,

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:24.918
and he came on board.

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:28.721
We started, you know, sort of
circling the story together,

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:31.090
and suddenly we were financed.
And now here we are.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.726
It was all like --
like happenstance, it felt like.

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:36.095
-Did you -- when you
originally had the idea,

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:37.964
you weren't planning
on playing the part?

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:39.198
Is that correct?
-No, no.

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:40.566
-And then once you were
playing the part,

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:43.602
how important was it for you
to find a David Duchovny?

00:01:43.602 --> 00:01:45.138
-There's just two people
in the movie.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:46.272
-Yeah, well, there you go?
-That's it.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.440
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.043
So I had to have a partner,
and he is so,

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:52.011
you know,
there's just no one like him.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.379
-He's really unique.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.082
He was here yesterday
and he's been here a bunch,

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:59.719
and I really love him
because I just can't predict

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:00.987
what he's going to say.
-No, or how

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:02.155
he's going to say it.
-Yeah.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:03.389
-Like, how he's going to say,
and I think

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:06.726
the clo-- the closest
comparable person

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:09.262
is like Walter Matthau, I mean,
in the best possible way.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.930
Like, he can spin a line,
he can break your heart,

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.132
he can make you laugh.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:15.434
He -- and he's a great partner,
you know?

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:17.737
And really, we had each other
and he had a lot of patience.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.338
He took, like,
a big chance with me.

00:02:19.338 --> 00:02:22.975
I'm like a second time director
and he's, you know,

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:24.843
he's the person
we all know and love.

00:02:24.843 --> 00:02:26.579
-And he, yeah.
-Was he worried

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.214
that you weren't going to pay
any attention to him

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.982
because you were also
in the movie?

00:02:29.982 --> 00:02:31.584
-Because I was in the movie?
-As a director, he's like,

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.152
I'm worried that you
as a director

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:34.720
are going to pay
no attention to me.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.922
-I thought that was a little,
maybe a little bit of a worry.

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:38.924
But I have to say, like,
we didn't have playback

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:40.526
while we were shooting,
we weren't able to look.

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:42.294
-So you weren't watching.
You would shoot a scene,

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:43.496
and you wouldn't be able to go
and watch it, right?

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.698
-No, no, so in the edit room,
I was like,

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:49.636
"Who's that blonde like blob in
the movie with David Duchovny?"

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:51.904
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:53.439
So he had nothing
to worry about.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:56.542
-He was telling us that you
shot in a working airport,

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:58.211
mostly at night
when nobody was there,

00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:00.613
but you also had to face
the challenges of daytime

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:01.680
when there were
actually travelers.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.317
-Yes, we had
real travelers who really

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:05.585
didn't care at all about us.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:07.520
-Yeah.
This big apparatus,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.855
all this light,
all this -- all this commotion.

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:11.057
And they didn't --
they didn't care much,

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.492
but it made us
have to be on our toes.

00:03:13.492 --> 00:03:16.361
We did eight minute long takes,
which for a movie actor,

00:03:16.361 --> 00:03:17.930
you just don't do that.
-Yeah.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.533
-So, and the great thing about
shooting in an airport at night

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.935
is that, well, eventually
no one's there.

00:03:22.935 --> 00:03:25.071
-Yeah.
-So we just felt like

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:26.338
we had the place to ourselves.

00:03:26.338 --> 00:03:28.007
We made a movie
kind of in secret,

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:30.509
and now it's just like, weird
feeling putting a movie out.

00:03:30.509 --> 00:03:33.713
It's like sending
your first born child out,

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:35.815
like, or your
or your oddest child out.

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:37.317
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:39.585
And just saying, "You just go be
yourself, honey."

00:03:39.585 --> 00:03:41.153
-We thought you'd stay at home,

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:42.822
but you are going
to go out in the world.

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:45.191
-Time to just go
out there and be yourself.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:46.625
-This is your second film
you've directed.

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:48.795
Is there -- is there some
element of filmmaking

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:51.630
that you enjoy
the most that surprised you?

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.866
-Well, you're just in on it
with everybody,

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.134
which is great
because you're working with

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.202
all these different kinds
of brands of artists,

00:03:57.202 --> 00:04:00.673
these, you know,
masters of light and sound,

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.775
and your senses become
very like, heightened,

00:04:03.775 --> 00:04:06.045
you know, like it's
a heightened sensory experience

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.714
and you're in on it
with the other actors.

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:09.081
And a lot of times
you're just -- you just

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:10.816
know your own process,
you know?

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.885
But this -- this way,
you're just -- you're,

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:14.720
I don't know, you've just got
a secret with everybody.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.390
-I've always been so impressed
when I stopped by sets,

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:18.791
when friends of mine
are directing a film,

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:21.294
there's just a thousand
choices a day.

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:23.061
Like, that's the part
you underestimate

00:04:23.061 --> 00:04:25.698
is you think a director
is just focused on the words

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.567
and these two great actors,

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:30.569
and then someone comes over
with like 30 spoons

00:04:30.569 --> 00:04:31.971
and they're like,
"Which spoon?"

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.172
-And, yeah, you could --
-Yeah.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.173
-You're right.

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:35.808
And this was like
I directed --

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:38.177
There I am, like, I --
I had to do so much,

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:40.212
I had to be so concentrated.

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:42.415
I had to really prepare,
prepare, prepare, prepare.

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:44.182
I was there for
five weeks beforehand.

00:04:44.182 --> 00:04:45.985
And then really it was

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.620
so I could just not think
about it with David,

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.523
so I could just
kind of be loose.

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.925
And my character
is like a, you know,

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:55.494
she's just a woo-woo,
very flaky person.

00:04:55.494 --> 00:04:58.731
And as a -- and as a director,
you have to really micromanage.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:00.767
So I kind of -- every time
they said action,

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:01.967
I was like, "Alright,
like, I'm just going

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:03.169
to relax a little bit.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:04.604
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.604 --> 00:05:07.373
Just be here with Mr. Duchovny."

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:09.508
-You dedicated this film
to Nora Ephron,

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:11.043
who obviously is a director
you worked

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:15.281
with quite famously
to great result.

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:16.815
What did you learn
most from her?

00:05:16.815 --> 00:05:18.017
Because obviously
you've done films

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:19.552
with so many different
kinds of directors.

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:22.822
-From her,
I just learned the pure delight.

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:24.991
She had pure delight
in moviemaking.

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:27.793
And the the thing about writing
and directing is that,

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:31.330
you know, the words come
to you in a -- it's so private.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.566
You know, your home, like,
it's 5:00 in the morning

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:36.902
and you run the scene in your
head and you write it down,

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:38.370
and then all of a sudden
you cut to the set

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.907
and there's 150 people who care
about bringing that to life

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:45.545
and have opinions about that,
and it's a joyful thing.

00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:49.448
And she had like the joy of
giving this great dinner party

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:50.816
every time you were on.

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:52.284
The sets with her
were really fun.

00:05:52.284 --> 00:05:53.686
-That's great.
-I heard Hugh Grant

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:55.387
say one time,
I didn't personally hear it,

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:57.990
but I read that he said doing

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.959
a comedy is like being a balloon
in a world of pins.

00:06:00.959 --> 00:06:02.328
-Uh-huh.
-That's true.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.364
And you just don't want the set
to be a world of pins.

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:06.198
-Yeah, that's a
good way of looking.

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:09.067
You -- you --
It's trick or treating.

00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:11.570
It's It's Halloween,
it's New York City.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.240
I actually have a son who's
going to be a vampire today.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.340
-Oh, you do?
-A five-year-old son

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:16.341
is going to be a vampire.

00:06:16.341 --> 00:06:17.910
This is your son, Jack.
-Yes.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.779
-When he was six?
-He was about six years old.

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:22.515
-And this is, obviously,
he has --

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:24.217
he comes
from show business stock.

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:25.918
His makeup is that good?
-Yeah.

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:27.353
He was so interested.
He was very --

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:28.754
-My five-year-old
looks like [bleep]

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:30.756
compared to him.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.756 --> 00:06:32.791
I should know your son,
Jack Quaid, he's a great actor.

00:06:32.791 --> 00:06:34.159
He's also been a guest
on the show.

00:06:34.159 --> 00:06:35.527
I'm a big fan of his, we are.
-He's so funny.

00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:37.864
This -- he wanted --
he wanted to be Dracula,

00:06:37.864 --> 00:06:39.732
but then he told me
like he want --

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:43.836
he had a name and he wanted
to be Count Jackula.

00:06:43.836 --> 00:06:45.237
-Count Jackula?
-Yeah.

00:06:45.237 --> 00:06:48.707
-Yeah.
--I was like, no, man.

00:06:48.707 --> 00:06:50.942
You just have to trust me
on this one.

00:06:50.942 --> 00:06:53.479
-They're already like
seven of those on the subway.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:54.881
-No, no.
-Yeah.

00:06:54.881 --> 00:06:57.083
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.083 --> 00:06:58.484
-You don't --
and they don't like...

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:00.920
-You don't do that.
[ Applause ]

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.388
-Congratulations on the film.

00:07:02.388 --> 00:07:03.922
It has just been so wonderful
having you here.

00:07:03.922 --> 00:07:04.990
Please come back soon.
-Thank you.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.759
-You guys, Meg Ryan.

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:08.461
"What Happens Later"
is in theatres Friday.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:09.929
We'll be right back
with Caitlin Collins.

